  "image_name": "20b981292a17cffe0e2437f30859e6de.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how an atmospheric river (AR) affects Antarctic weather across several scales, linking large-scale circulation to mountain and cyclone processes near the coast. Overall, it shows a long, narrow moisture airstream extending from lower latitudes toward Antarctica, carrying evaporated ocean moisture southward. In the lower and middle troposphere, this AR feeds into a coastal cyclone, where low-level convergence, rising motion, and precipitation strengthen the storm. Aloft, the jet stream and upper-level pressure pattern are connected to this process: divergence and lift seem to intensify the surface cyclone, while latent heat released by cloud and precipitation over Antarctica reinforces an upper-level high and helps amplify cyclogenesis.\n\nThe mountain panel highlights how the incoming AR interacts with Antarctic topography. On the windward side, mixed-phase cloud formation and latent heat release occur as air rises over the mountains, while the leeward side experiences descending, adiabatically warmed foehn flow. Another visible influence is downwelling longwave radiation associated with cloud cover.\n\nThe cyclone panel shows the AR embedded in frontal structure, with warm and cold fronts and a warm conveyor belt transporting moist air upward toward the interior and coastal mountains, helping organize heavy precipitation near the cyclone.",
